{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1160,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The International Astronomical Union defines\nBrown Dwarfs as balls of gas in space that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6480,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are too small to be bona-fide hydrogen-burning\nstars, but large enough to burn deuterium,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11880,
    "dDurationMs": 3749,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which anything bigger than about 13 times\nthe mass of Jupiter can do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15629,
    "dDurationMs": 4041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because of this, brown dwarfs are often called\n“failed stars” or “super Jupiters.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19670,
    "dDurationMs": 5109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, there’s a major problem with this\ndeuterium-burning-based definition: it doesn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24779,
    "dDurationMs": 1871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make any scientific sense."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26650,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, unlike how hydrogen fusion is huge\nsince it means you can shine brightly for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31390,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "millions or billions of years, burning deuterium\ndoesn’t appear to affect an astronomical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36150,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object in any particularly meaningful way,\nwhich is probably why you haven’t heard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39820,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much about it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40820,
    "dDurationMs": 4579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, on this density vs mass plot, hydrogen\nburning is a cutoff that clearly distinguishes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45399,
    "dDurationMs": 8071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars from non-hydrogen-burning things, while\ndeuterium burning doesn’t appear distinguishing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53470,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54470,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it may seem like there is no distinction\nbetween things that are slightly-too-small"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57550,
    "dDurationMs": 4111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be stars (which we call brown dwarfs) and\ngiant gas planets, and that they’re all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61661,
    "dDurationMs": 2349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really the same kind of object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64010,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, just because deuterium isn’t a\ngood cutoff doesn’t mean there aren’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67550,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other options."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68550,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, let’s briefly list the features that\nDO scientifically distinguish brown dwarf-like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72830,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects from gas-giant-like objects (And a\ncaveat here: some of these statements are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77450,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still being debated within the astronomical\ncommunity, but for each one there are at least"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81330,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some researchers arguing in favor of it):"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84500,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85500,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Movement: Brown dwarfs (whether above or below\nthe deuterium limit) and stars appear to be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89550,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "located and move in similar ways: in loose\nclusters with other similar objects moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94210,
    "dDurationMs": 1790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with roughly the same relative speeds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96000,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planets, on the other hand, move around stars\nin orbits, and are much closer to the nearest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102190,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star – which can even be a brown dwarf."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105120,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106120,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Formation: Brown dwarfs (whether above or\nbelow the deuterium limit) and stars appear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107120,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to follow the same distribution of masses,\nsuggesting they form the same way: the gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111060,
    "dDurationMs": 2890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse of a cloud of gas and dust."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113950,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planets appear to follow a different distribution\nof masses, suggesting they form in their own"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117750,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way: by accreting from the protoplanetary\ndisk of gas and dust leftover around a star"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122450,
    "dDurationMs": 3270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(or brown dwarf) after it forms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125720,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126720,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Metallicity: The dust and gas leftover from\nstar formation has higher concentrations of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130910,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metal, so the atmospheres of gas giant planets\nhave elevated levels of metal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135560,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brown dwarfs (whether above or below the deuterium\nlimit) have around the same amount of metal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138330,
    "dDurationMs": 1240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139570,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "4."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140570,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Size of Orbits: Protoplanetary disks around\nstars typically don’t extend much farther"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145440,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than a few hundred times the distance between\nthe earth and the sun, so that’s about as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148730,
    "dDurationMs": 1750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far out as you find planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150480,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, brown dwarfs (whether above or below\nthe deuterium limit) often orbit stars or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153050,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other brown dwarfs in binary pairs at significantly\ngreater distances ."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156090,
    "dDurationMs": 1340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "5."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157430,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mass Ratio: Protoplanetary disks are pretty\nmuch never more than 10% of the mass of their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162130,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parent star (or brown dwarf), so a planet-to-star\nmass ratio is almost always more extreme than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166770,
    "dDurationMs": 1290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1 to 10."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168060,
    "dDurationMs": 2540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, brown dwarfs (whether above or below\nthe deuterium limit) and stars regularly orbit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170600,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in pairs with mass ratios much closer to 1\nto 1, suggesting they formed from their own"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174580,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clouds of gas and dust."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175580,
    "dDurationMs": 3030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Basically, a lot of evidence points to two\nseparate populations of objects: things that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178610,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form from gravitationally collapsing clouds\nof gas, and things that form from the leftovers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183590,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It appears an unfortunate coincidence that\nthe overlap in these two populations is roughly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188040,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the mass where deuterium-burning becomes\npossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190680,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, IF we didn’t have any other good\nways to distinguish between brown dwarfs and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194810,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planets, sure, deuterium burning might be\na reasonable rule of thumb."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197940,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s also possible, as some researchers\ncontend, that there IS no real, clear way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202340,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of distinguishing between brown dwarfs and\ngiant planets, and that they really do just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206080,
    "dDurationMs": 1300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist on a spectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207380,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But either way, deuterium is more or less\na distraction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210780,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, among those who think that the evidence\nsuggests brown dwarfs are different from giant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214770,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planets, what supposedly distinguishes them\nis how they formed, their consequent behavior,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219530,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their composition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220650,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The claim is this: planets, no matter how\nbig, appear to be the leftovers of star formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226050,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And brown dwarfs, no matter how small, appear\nto be failed stars: they started off the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230870,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ways stars do by gravitationally collapsing\nfrom a cloud of dust, but failed to capture"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235570,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough mass to burn hydrogen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237390,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps in the end it doesn’t matter how\nbadly they failed – that is, it doesn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241480,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter if they also can’t burn deuterium\n– what matters is that they aspired to be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245810,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars, and fell short."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250270,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to NASA’s James Webb Space Telescope\nProject at the Space Telescope Science Institute"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254510,
    "dDurationMs": 1499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for supporting this video."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256009,
    "dDurationMs": 5181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JWST is literally the perfect telescope for\nstudying brown dwarfs: it sees best in infrared"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261190,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light, and guess what brown dwarfs mostly\nemit: yup, infrared!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265690,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unlike stars which are super hot, the smallest\nbrown dwarfs are about the same temperature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269560,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as you and me, and they give off infrared\nlight right in the middle of JWST’s spectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275820,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This also means that if you and I were in\nspace, we’d shine out like a beacon to JWST"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280350,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even with no stars nearby."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282210,
    "dDurationMs": 6690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So JWST will be able to find and study human-temperature\nbrown dwarfs and compare them with super Jupiters"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288900,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to help settle the brown dwarf debate."
    } ]
  } ]
}
